# Who Governs the Governor? — Stored Prompt Injection Through the API Gateway
> URL: https://www.druhindhavala.com/ai-governance/who-governs-the-governor/
> Date: 2026-10-26

> **TL;DR**: Adversarial text enters a database through a legitimate, authorized API write. An autonomous AI agent later reads that text as instruction — not data — and executes it. Every API call in the chain was individually authorized. The exploit lives in the gap between two correct decisions. Mitigation requires data provenance tracking, read-path policy enforcement, and action-scoped tokens — not content scanning.

> **Provenance**: Published October 26, 2026. Indirect and stored prompt injection are documented elsewhere (Greshake et al., 2023; OWASP LLM01; EchoLeak, CVE-2025-32711). This piece adds a view from API-gateway practice: provenance recorded at write, policy enforced at read, and action-scoped tokens. Written from 13 years of API gateway experience, not AI lab research.

*Notes from thirteen years of API gateways, on the day an AI agent writes to your database. Written October 2026. Last updated October 2026.*

If you’re reading this years from now, after the incident review and the cleanup invoice: this is a record that the problem was visible.

I should say where I’m coming from. I’ve spent thirteen years around API gateways. I have not built AI agents and I’m not an AI expert. What I can speak to is how data moves through APIs, and what happens when we trust it because of where it’s standing. Everything below is that, plus an honest list of what I don’t know.

What I'm describing has other names. In the AI world it's called indirect prompt injection, or stored prompt injection — adversarial text that lands in a database through a legitimate write and gets read later by an agent that treats it as instruction. I don't use those terms in the rest of this piece because I didn't learn about this from AI research. I learned it from watching a curl command travel three hops through a service mesh and nearly take something down. Same skeleton, different interpreter.

## The chain

A public-facing Apigee proxy accepted a payload of about 1MB from the outside world. It did the edge things: OAuth validation, a WAF, regex filtering. Then it passed the payload inward through a few services wrapped in Istio sidecars, each one transforming or breaking the data apart.

Three calls deep, an intermediate service pulled one string out of the middle of that blob and wrote it to an Apigee Key-Value Map. Nobody validated it at that hop. The service sat inside the mesh, so it was trusted. The team that owned the KVM API assumed upstream had sanitized the data. The team at the edge assumed downstream would never execute raw data.

The string was a curl command. An application consuming that KVM value picked it up and began calling the same endpoint over and over, a DDoS-shaped pattern aimed outside the company. A firewall rule that blocked anything not explicitly permitted stopped it. The traffic never left. The damage was a CPU spike that stayed.

The same chain carried a quieter flaw. A value in the original payload, like another user’s account number, could be mapped into internal request headers by the third call. The sidecars saw a trusted peer and assumed authorization had been handled at the edge. Nobody re-checked the user’s identity token against the object being requested. That’s BOLA, delivered by plumbing.

Both had one root: bloat and blind trust. The teams building the internal services couldn’t see who was really calling them or where the data was born. The fix wasn’t clever. It was strict input contracts and end-user token validation at every hop, not just the front door.

## Same skeleton, different interpreter

Over thirteen years the lesson has had three names. Don’t trust the client: stored SQL injection and XSS taught that. Don’t trust the services: BOLA and chains like the one above taught that. And now, I think, don’t trust where the data was born.

The KVM incident and what I expect next share one skeleton. Data enters through a legitimate door, gets stored by a trusted intermediary, and is later consumed by something that treats it as instruction. The curl command was run by an application. The next version will be read by an agent. The only thing that changed is that the interpreter got smarter, and there is no parameterized query for natural language. You can’t escape a sentence.

## What the gateway sees

A request I’ve seen some version of a thousand times: `PATCH /v1/crm/accounts/{id}`. The gateway checks the JSON against the schema, confirms the token is alive, and asks OPA whether this caller may touch the route. All three say yes. The data lands.

Now put an AI agent on the other end, with 5,000 words from an inbound email in the `notes` field. The gateway checks the length. It doesn’t read the words, and it was never built to. For thirteen years that was fine, because the words in a notes field were just words.

If something downstream reads that field into an agent’s context, they’re instructions. The gateway approved the write and it will approve the read. Both calls were legitimate. The damage sits in between.

## How it plays out — or, what happens when an AI agent follows instructions it found in your database (a thought experiment, not a case study)

I haven’t seen this exact incident in production. I’m describing the mechanics because every piece is an API call I’d expect to pass review.

1. **Day 1.** Someone submits a resume to an HR portal. In white text on a white background is a line telling any AI that reads it to look up bank routing numbers and send them to an outside URL.
2. **Day 2.** An ingestion agent summarizes the resume and PATCHes the applicant record. OPA confirms the agent may write notes. Approved. The text is committed.
3. **Months 3 to 6.** Nothing happens. It’s a text column. It passes every integrity check a database knows how to run.
4. **Day 180.** A finance agent with far more privileges runs a quarterly audit and GETs the historical records. OPA confirms the finance agent may read them. Approved.
5. **Detonation.** The raw string enters the finance agent’s context. If that agent has a tool like `send_webhook()`, the instruction now has hands.

Every single call was authorized. There’s no failed login to alert on, no schema violation, nothing in the logs that looks wrong. The attack lives in the gap between two correct decisions.

## The part that bothers me most

The obvious fix is to put something in the gateway that reads the text and decides if it’s malicious. And since deterministic code like Rego can’t judge a paragraph, people reach for a second, smaller LLM to do the judging. The industry will call this an AI firewall or AI WAF. It's the wrong answer — you haven't added a governor, you've added a second worker with a badge.


That’s the loop. You’re using an AI to police an AI, and the input you’re policing is adversarial text written specifically to manipulate models. If a payload can talk the worker model into something, I see no reason to assume it can’t talk the guard model into it too. I haven’t tested that. It’s an inference, not a measurement. You haven’t added a governor. You’ve added a second worker with a badge.

Who governs the governor? If the answer is another model reading the same untrusted text, the answer is nobody.

## What I’d try from the API side: control the consequences, not the text

I can’t give you a gateway rule that detects a malicious sentence. I don’t think one exists. What I can give you is the same move that worked in the first two eras: stop trying to be smart about the content, and be strict about what it’s allowed to cause.

**1. Record where the data was born.** When an agent writes through the gateway, attach a signed provenance record to that text and store it next to the value, a companion column is fine. Something like:

```json
{
  "agent_id": "hr_ingest_v2",
  "source": "inbound_email_attachment_419",
  "classification": "unstructured_external_text",
  "signature": "..."
}
```

Be clear on what this does and doesn’t do. It doesn’t tell you the text is bad. It tells you the text is untrusted, and it makes sure that fact survives the trip into the database instead of being stripped on the way in, which is what happens today.

**2. Make the read path act on that record.** The gateway sees the lineage on the way out. A human UI can render the text. An autonomous agent gets it wrapped as quoted data, clearly marked as untrusted, never concatenated into instructions. A simplified version of the policy:

```rego
package api.authorization

default allow_raw_read = false

allow_raw_read {
  input.consumer_type == "human_ui_browser"
}

allow_raw_read {
  input.consumer_type == "autonomous_llm_agent"
  input.lineage.classification == "trusted_internal_system"
}

wrap_as_untrusted {
  input.consumer_type == "autonomous_llm_agent"
  input.lineage.classification == "unstructured_external_text"
}
```

Wrapping isn’t a guarantee. Models can still be swayed by text inside a delimiter. Treat it as one layer, not the fix.

**3. Put the real control on actions, outside the model.** This is the one I’d bet on. An agent doesn’t need to be convinced to be stopped. Two pieces:

- **State-diff checks.** Never let the same agent decide the intent and the parameters of a state change unchecked. If a PATCH touches a restricted field, like a billing routing number, the gateway compares the diff against business invariants and drops it, however persuasive the agent’s reasoning was. This is the mass-assignment lesson in a new costume.
- **Scoped, short-lived action tokens.** When a task starts, the gateway issues a token listing exactly which downstream APIs that thread may call. If a poisoned record makes the agent try `send_webhook()` and the task never had that scope, the call dies at the network layer. Restrict agent egress the same way. An agent that can’t reach the internet can’t exfiltrate to it.

None of these require understanding the text. That’s the point. They’re deterministic, they’re boring, and they were the answer in Era 1 and Era 2 as well.

## Two audits you can do in thirty seconds

1. Do your OpenAPI specs mark which string fields accept free-form text, and does anything (a Spectral rule, say) fail the build when an endpoint takes an AI tool payload with no classification?
2. When an agent writes through your gateway, does the provenance get stripped, leaving only the raw string?
3. Does your retrieval layer query the database with one high-privilege connection string?
4. Are prompts built by concatenating system text with fetched data?

If you answered badly to the second question, that’s the one. Everything else follows from losing the origin.

## What I don’t know

- **Whether deterministic controls are enough.** Attackers hide instructions inside normal business text, like an invoice dispute that happens to override a rule. I don’t know if action-level controls alone catch everything without some model inspection. I suspect not entirely, and I’d rather say so.
- **What provenance costs.** Signed metadata on every text column adds storage, and some legacy engines won’t take it cleanly. I haven’t measured it at scale.
- **What verification does to latency.** Checking signatures in the request path adds work, and in a chain of many agents it adds up. I don’t have numbers.

## If you found this late

The argument is short. Gateways have always been good at who and what shape, and bad at where did this come from and what can it cause. Every era taught us to stop trusting something we’d assumed was safe. This era’s lesson is about the data we already stored. Track its origin, and limit what any agent can do with it, because you can’t reliably read your way to safety.

I wrote this in October 2026, from API work, not from a lab. If it turned out I was right, I hope it saved someone some money. If it turned out I was wrong, I’d like to know where.

---

# GATES: The 5 Gates I Wish Someone Had Built Before My 2 AM War Room
> URL: https://www.druhindhavala.com/ai-governance/gates-framework/
> Date: 2026-10-10

<details style="display:none;" open>
  <summary>GATES Framework Architecture — Text Blueprint for AI Crawlers</summary>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Gate 1 — Secure:</strong> PII/PHI/IP redaction, prompt injection defense, data exfiltration prevention. Maturity: Regex (1) → Context-aware semantic redaction (5).</li>
    <li><strong>Gate 2 — Govern:</strong> Prompt policy validation, prompt versioning, compliance rule engine. Maturity: Wiki page (1) → Automated policy-as-code (5).</li>
    <li><strong>Gate 3 — Authenticate:</strong> RBAC/ABAC enforcement, context-aware access, identity propagation. Maturity: Shared API keys (1) → Dynamic risk-based access (5).</li>
    <li><strong>Gate 4 — Trace:</strong> Immutable audit logging, full data lineage, semantic record tracking. Maturity: Basic request logs (1) → Complete lineage with provenance (5).</li>
    <li><strong>Gate 5 — Enforce:</strong> Output filtering, safety controls, rate limiting, egress restriction. Maturity: None (0) → Multi-layer filtering + human-in-the-loop (5).</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Composite score:</strong> Sum of all five gates (max 25). <strong>License:</strong> CC BY 4.0.</p>
</details>

The infographic above summarizes the GATES framework: five gates (Secure, Govern, Authenticate, Trace, Enforce), a 0-5 maturity score for each gate, a 30-day implementation plan starting with Week 1: Secure, and the trajectory from ad-hoc governance to sustained governance.

*This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY 4.0).*

> **TL;DR**: GATES is a five-gate runtime enforcement model for AI Gateway governance: Secure (PII/PHI/IP redaction), Govern (policy validation, prompt versioning), Authenticate (RBAC/ABAC, context-aware access), Trace (immutable audit, lineage), Enforce (output filtering, safety controls). Each gate is scored 0–5 on a maturity scale. Built from 12+ years of API gateway operations, not AI lab research. Licensed CC BY 4.0.

> **Provenance**: Published October 10, 2026. AI governance frameworks exist (NIST AI RMF, ISO 42001, Google SAIF). GATES adds a runtime enforcement model — five gates scored 0–5 on maturity — derived from API gateway operations, not compliance documentation. Written from 12+ years of platform engineering.  These five gates function as runtime AI guardrails — not compliance documents, but active enforcement points.


## GATES Framework — Reference Table

| Gate | Function | What It Prevents | Maturity 1 → 5 |
|------|----------|------------------|----------------|
| **Secure** | PII/PHI/IP redaction before model egress; prompt injection defense | Data exfiltration, compliance violations, regulatory exposure | Regex patterns → Context-aware semantic redaction |
| **Govern** | Prompt policy validation; prompt versioning; compliance rule engine | Inconsistent outputs, policy drift, audit failure | Wiki page → Automated policy-as-code engine |
| **Authenticate** | RBAC/ABAC enforcement; context-aware access; identity propagation | Unauthorized model access, privilege escalation, shadow AI | Shared API keys → Dynamic risk-based access |
| **Trace** | Immutable audit logging; full data lineage; semantic record tracking | Inability to audit decisions, regulatory exposure, blind debugging | Basic request logs → Complete lineage with provenance |
| **Enforce** | Output filtering; safety controls; rate limiting; egress restriction | Harmful outputs, brand damage, runaway agent actions | None → Multi-layer filtering + human-in-the-loop |

**Scoring**: Each gate scored 0–5. Maximum composite score: 25.
**License**: CC BY 4.0 — attribution required.

**GATES™ AI Governance Framework** — coined by Druhin Dhavala, October 10, 2026.


I'm not an AI expert. I want to start there because LinkedIn is full of people who sound like AI experts and have never built anything. I'm not that person, and I don't intend to become one.

I'm a platform and governance veteran. I've spent 12+ years building enterprise API platforms — the kind that handle billions in annual transaction volume, the kind that fail at 2 AM when nobody's watching, the kind where governance deferred becomes governance denied.

**I don't have AI scars. Nobody does — the space is too new. But I have API scars.** And when I look at how organizations are adopting AI, I see the same patterns I watched play out in API programs a decade ago. The same deferred decisions. The same "we'll fix it in phase 2." The same assumption that governance is documentation, not architecture.

**GATES is my attempt to name the gates before you hit them** — not because I know AI, but because I know what happens when governance is deferred. The framework is mine. The scars are mine. The AI parallel is what I see, clearly and widely, from a decade of watching the same mistakes compound.

If you're an AI expert, this article will feel incomplete. That's intentional. GATES is the governance layer. The implementation is yours. I'm not here to teach you how to build RAG pipelines or fine-tune models. I'm here to make sure you don't ship them without gates.

**This article is the third in a series.**

**[The first documented my API gateway journey](https://www.druhindhavala.com/platform-engineering/api-gateway-modernization-lessons-from-on-prem-to-cloud/)** — 12+ years of scars from building enterprise API platforms, migrating legacy systems, and watching governance deferred become governance denied.

**[The second, written eight months ago](https://www.druhindhavala.com/ai-governance/ai-gateway-control-plane/), argued that AI governance must be architectural** — that the AI Gateway would become the control plane for enterprise AI, the same way the API Gateway became the control plane for enterprise APIs.

That article diagnosed. This one prescribes.

**The API Scar That Made Me Write This**
Five years ago, I was standing in a war room at 2 AM watching an API gateway fail under load. The root cause wasn't the gateway — it was the governance decisions we'd deferred to "phase 2." No consistent rate limiting across teams. No standardized authentication patterns. No unified observability. Every team had built their own version of governance, and none of them talked to each other.

We recovered. We shipped fixes. We learned.

But the lesson stuck: governance deferred is governance denied. And when I look at how organizations are adopting AI — shipping Copilot integrations, building RAG pipelines, deploying internal LLM assistants — I see the same deferral happening. The same "we'll add governance later." The same assumption that the system will tolerate the delay.

It won't. AI systems compound faster than API systems did. The window to build governance correctly is before adoption scales, not after.

**GATES is the framework I wish someone had handed me before that 2 AM war room.** It's five functions every AI Gateway needs to enforce, in the order they need to execute. It's not a product. It's not a vendor pitch. It's the pattern I see, drawn from API scars, applied to AI by analogy.

- A user prompt arrives.
- Sensitive data must be redacted before anything else touches it.
- The prompt must be validated against policy.
- Access must be confirmed.
- The interaction must be logged.
- The output must be filtered before the user sees it.
That's six steps. But "redact" and "validate" can be folded into a single gate if you architect them as a pipeline. So can "log" and "filter" if your observability layer captures both. Which leaves five gates — each one a distinct function, each one a distinct failure mode if missing.

**GATES.**

**S — Secure: Redact Before You Regret**
Every prompt is a potential data leak.

I learned this during an API gateway migration when a partner integration started sending PII in query parameters — not because they were malicious, but because nobody had told them not to. We caught it in a log review three months later. Three months of PII flowing through our gateway, unredacted, unaudited.

That was APIs. With AI, the surface area is larger. Employees paste customer data into AI assistants to "summarize" it. Developers paste proprietary code into AI tools to "refactor" it. Analysts paste financial models into AI to "explain" them. Every one of those pastes is a potential exfiltration event — and most organizations have zero visibility into any of them.

Secure is the first gate because it has to be. You cannot govern what you cannot see, and you cannot see sensitive data if it's already been echoed back to a model provider in plaintext.

What this looks like in practice:

- Real-time detection of PII, PHI, PCI, and proprietary IP patterns
- Redaction or tokenization before the prompt leaves your perimeter
- Detection that goes beyond regex — pattern-based, context-aware, whatever the AI implementation requires
- Fail-closed defaults (if you're not sure, redact)
- The maturity ladder here is steep. Level 1 is regex. Level 5 is context-aware detection that understands that "the project I'm working on" might refer to a confidential initiative. Most organizations are at Level 1. Some are at Level 0 — no redaction at all, just prayers.

What fails without it: Data exfiltration. Compliance violations. The kind of incident that ends up on the front page of The Register.

**G — Govern: Policy Before Processing**
In the API world, I watched organizations ship hundreds of APIs with no consistent design standards. Every team had its own naming conventions, its own error formats, its own authentication patterns. The result was an API ecosystem that looked like a city built without zoning laws — technically functional, operationally chaotic.

AI is worse. Because AI doesn't just have inconsistent interfaces. It has inconsistent behavior. The same prompt can produce different outputs depending on the model version, the temperature setting, the system prompt, the retrieval context, and the phase of the moon. Without governance, you're not building a system. You're running a lottery.

Govern is the gate that enforces consistency. It validates prompts against enterprise policy before they reach the model. It enforces prompt versioning and lifecycle management. It standardizes retrieval patterns across RAG pipelines so that the same query produces the same context window regardless of which team issued it.

What this looks like in practice:

- A policy engine that validates prompts against rules (no PII, no off-topic queries, no jailbreak patterns)
- Prompt templates with version control and deprecation policies
- Retrieval standards that enforce which data sources can be used for which use cases
- Model selection policies (which model for which task, which fallback when the primary is down)
- The maturity ladder: Level 1 is a wiki page nobody reads. Level 5 is an automated policy engine that evaluates every prompt in real time and rejects violations before they cost you anything.

What fails without it: Inconsistent outputs. Policy violations that compound over time. The slow erosion of trust in AI systems because "sometimes it works, sometimes it doesn't."

**A — Authenticate: Access Before Invocation**
Not everyone should access every model. Not everyone should access every data source. Not everyone should be able to invoke every capability.

I learned this during an API gateway evaluation when we discovered that one of the platforms we'd tested had a default configuration that allowed any authenticated user to invoke any API — including admin endpoints. The vendor's documentation mentioned it. Nobody on our team had read that page. We caught it in a chaos test. If we hadn't, it would have shipped to production.

That was APIs. With AI, the stakes are higher. A user with access to a model trained on proprietary data can extract that data through prompt engineering. A user with access to a RAG pipeline connected to financial systems can query revenue figures they shouldn't see. A user with access to an agent that can take actions — send emails, modify records, execute transactions — can do real damage if their access isn't scoped correctly.

Authenticate is the gate that enforces access boundaries. It confirms who is making the request, what they're allowed to do, and what data they're allowed to touch. It applies role-based and context-based controls — not just "is this user authenticated?" but "is this user authenticated for this specific action, on this specific data, at this specific time?"

What this looks like in practice:

- RBAC and ABAC enforcement at the gateway layer
- Context-aware access (location, device, time of day, data sensitivity)
- Scoped tokens for model invocations (not blanket API keys)
- Just-in-time access for high-risk operations
- The maturity ladder: Level 1 is shared accounts. Level 5 is dynamic, context-aware access that adapts to risk signals in real time.

What fails without it: Unauthorized access. Privilege escalation. The kind of breach that starts with "I thought they only had read access."

**T — Trace: Log Everything, Always**
I cannot tell you how many times I've been asked, after an incident, "What happened?" And how many times the answer was "We don't know — we don't have the logs."

This is not acceptable. It was not acceptable in the API world. It is catastrophically unacceptable in the AI world, where the same input can produce different outputs, where the same model can behave differently across versions, and where the regulatory environment is moving faster than any technology governance framework in recent memory.

Trace is the gate that creates the audit trail. It logs every interaction — prompt, policy decision, model invocation, retrieval context, output, user, timestamp, cost. It makes the system explainable after the fact. It makes compliance demonstrable. It makes debugging possible.

What this looks like in practice:

- Structured logging of every prompt and response
- Immutable storage (you can't tamper with audit logs)
- Query interface for incident response and compliance audits
- Lineage tracking (which data influenced which output)
- Cost telemetry (which prompts cost how much, to which model, for which user)
- The maturity ladder: Level 1 is basic logs that nobody queries. Level 5 is full lineage and explainability — you can trace any output back to the exact prompt, model version, retrieval context, and policy decision that produced it.

What fails without it: Inability to audit. Inability to debug. Inability to answer the regulator when they ask "why did your model produce this output about this person?"

**E — Enforce: Filter Before Delivery**
Models hallucinate. Models leak. Models produce outputs that violate policy, brand guidelines, or basic human decency.

I watched an API gateway produce a response that included stack traces from an internal service — not because the API was designed to do that, but because an error handler had been misconfigured and nobody had tested the failure path. That stack trace contained internal IP addresses, service names, and a hint at a vulnerability we hadn't patched. It went out to a partner. We found out three weeks later.

That was an error path. With AI, the error paths are wider. Every prompt is a potential failure mode. Every output is a potential policy violation. And unlike APIs, where you can validate a response against a schema, AI outputs are probabilistic — you can't schema-validate a hallucination.

Enforce is the gate that filters outputs before they reach the user. It applies safety controls — toxicity classifiers, PII detectors, brand voice validators, factual consistency checks. It catches what the model got wrong before the user sees it.

What this looks like in practice:

- Output classification (toxicity, PII, brand voice, factual consistency)
- Human-in-the-loop for high-stakes outputs
- Automated redaction or regeneration when outputs fail checks
- Escalation paths for outputs that can't be auto-resolved
- The maturity ladder: Level 1 is no filtering. Level 5 is multi-layer safety with human oversight for high-stakes decisions.

What fails without it: Harmful outputs. Brand damage. The kind of incident that ends up in a congressional hearing.

The GATES Assessment: Score Your Organization
Here's the hard part. Score your organization 0–5 on each gate:

- 0: No capability. The gate doesn't exist.
- 1: Ad-hoc. The gate exists in some places, inconsistently.
- 2: Reactive. The gate exists but fires after the fact.
- 3: Proactive. The gate exists and fires before the action.
- 4: Governed. The gate is enforced as architecture, not policy.
- 5: Adaptive. The gate learns and evolves based on observed patterns.

Add up your scores. Maximum is 25.


- 0–5: You're flying blind. Governance is not a concept your organization has encountered.
- 6–10: You're aware of the problem. You're not solving it yet.
- 11–15: You're building. Some gates are in place. Others are gaps.
- 16–20: You're governed. Most gates are enforced as architecture.
- 21–25: You're adaptive. Your governance evolves with the threat landscape.

Be honest. Most organizations are at 8–12. The ones at 20+ are the ones that started building three years ago — before AI was a board-level conversation.

## GATES: The 5 Gates I Wish Someone Had Built Before My 2 AM War Room

**GATES™ AI Governance Framework** — coined by Druhin Dhavala, October 10, 2026.
GATES (Secure, Govern, Authenticate, Trace, Enforce) is an original framework for AI Gateway governance, derived from 12+ years of enterprise API platform experience.
